/* =============================================================================
   NexusDL TUI — global theme
   =============================================================================
   This file is the App's CSS, loaded by Textual via the ``CSS_PATH``
   class attribute on :class:`nexusdl.interfaces.cli.app.NexusDLApp`.

   It is **layer 2** of the TUI's styling:

     • Layer 1 — Textual's own default stylesheet and the active theme
       (``textual-dark`` by default, from ``settings.tui.theme``). This
       provides the theme variables (``$primary``, ``$surface``,
       ``$accent``, …) and the baseline styling every widget inherits.

     • Layer 2 — this file. Extends the theme with app-specific styling
       for standard widgets (Input, Button, OptionList, DataTable,
       Select), the modal overlay, scrollbars, and the App's Footer
       and Header.

     • Layer 3 — the per-widget ``DEFAULT_CSS`` declared on each widget
       and screen. Widget-specific rules that only make sense inside
       that widget.

   The layering means this file does **not** override the widget-level
   rules; it only adds what those rules assume exists (theme variables,
   standard widget appearance, global chrome). A rule that would
   conflict with a widget's own ``DEFAULT_CSS`` belongs on the widget,
   not here.

   Theme variables come from the active Textual theme. Overriding them
   here would defeat the purpose of ``settings.tui.theme`` — a user
   picking ``textual-light`` expects the palette to switch. Custom
   variables (prefixed ``$nsdl-``) are allowed for values that are
   genuinely app-specific and not part of the theme contract.
   ============================================================================= */


/* =============================================================================
   Custom variables
   =============================================================================
   These are **preparatory**, not yet consumed by any CSS rule in this
   file. The manga card and chapter list currently build their styled
   ``Text`` spans with literal Rich style strings (``"bold red"``,
   ``"bold cyan"``, ``"green"``) rather than theme variables. The
   intent of declaring the variables here is to have a single named
   home for those colours so a future refactor of the widget code can
   reference them symbolically instead of repeating the literals.

   A themer reading this file sees the app's "semantic colour
   palette" in one place, even though nothing in the CSS itself uses
   the variables yet. That is the trade-off: a small amount of
   currently-unused declarations versus a future widget refactor that
   has to hunt for the colour names across three widget files. The
   declarations are cheap and self-documenting.
   ============================================================================= */

$nsdl-adult-badge: $error;
$nsdl-js-badge: $warning;
$nsdl-status-current: $accent;
$nsdl-status-read: $success;


/* =============================================================================
   Screen base
   =============================================================================
   Every Screen in the app already declares its own ``background:
   $surface`` in its ``DEFAULT_CSS``. This rule catches Screens that do
   not (a future ModalScreen subclass, a dialog) so the app never
   renders a white flash on top of a dark theme.

   The base colour is ``$surface`` — the same as the screens' own
   ``DEFAULT_CSS`` — so a screen that fails to declare its background
   looks visually identical to one that does. A screen that wants the
   slightly darker ``$background`` (a future "full-screen video" or
   "minimal" mode) declares it explicitly in its own ``DEFAULT_CSS``.
   ============================================================================= */

Screen {
    background: $surface;
    color: $text;
}


/* =============================================================================
   Header and Footer
   =============================================================================
   The app does not push a Header (the main screen's own dashboard
   header is a Label), but the Footer is enabled for the whole app so
   every screen shows its bindings. Styling the Footer here keeps the
   shortcut hints visually consistent with the rest of the chrome.
   ============================================================================= */

Footer {
    background: $panel;
    color: $text-muted;
    height: 1;
}

Footer > .footer--key {
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold;
}

Footer > .footer--description {
    color: $text-muted;
}

Footer > .footer--highlight {
    background: $primary;
    color: $text;
}

Footer > .footer--highlight-key {
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold;
}

Header {
    background: $panel;
    color: $text;
    text-style: bold;
}


/* =============================================================================
   Input
   =============================================================================
   The search, library, and download screens each contain an Input. The
   border colour communicates focus: neutral primary when idle, accent
   when focused, error when the value is rejected. Textual's default is
   already close to this; the rules below tighten the padding and the
   border character selection so text inside the input sits flush with
   the surrounding chrome.
   ============================================================================= */

Input {
    background: $surface;
    color: $text;
    border: tall $primary;
    padding: 0 1;
}

Input:focus {
    border: tall $accent;
}

Input.-invalid,
Input.-invalid:focus {
    border: tall $error;
}

Input > .input--placeholder {
    color: $text-muted;
}

Input > .input--cursor {
    background: $accent;
    color: $surface;
}


/* =============================================================================
   Button
   =============================================================================
   Every screen in the app uses Button variants (``default``,
   ``success``, ``warning``, ``error``). Textual generates a
   ``Button.-{variant}`` class for each; styling the class once here
   means every call site's variant choice is honoured with the same
   visual language. The disabled state is uniform across variants —
   a disabled ``success`` button should not look green.
   ============================================================================= */

Button {
    min-width: 8;
    margin: 0 1 0 0;
    border: tall $primary;
    content-align: center middle;
}

Button.-default {
    background: $panel;
    color: $text;
    border: tall $primary;
}

Button.-default:hover {
    background: $boost;
    border: tall $accent;
}

Button.-default:focus {
    border: tall $accent;
}

Button.-primary {
    background: $primary;
    color: $text;
    border: tall $primary;
}

Button.-primary:hover {
    background: $primary-lighten-1;
}

Button.-primary:focus {
    border: tall $accent;
}

Button.-success {
    background: $success;
    color: $text;
    border: tall $success;
}

Button.-success:hover {
    background: $success-lighten-1;
}

Button.-success:focus {
    border: tall $accent;
}

Button.-warning {
    background: $warning;
    color: $text;
    border: tall $warning;
}

Button.-warning:hover {
    background: $warning-lighten-1;
}

Button.-warning:focus {
    border: tall $accent;
}

Button.-error {
    background: $error;
    color: $text;
    border: tall $error;
}

Button.-error:hover {
    background: $error-lighten-1;
}

Button.-error:focus {
    border: tall $accent;
}

Button:disabled {
    background: $panel;
    color: $text-disabled;
    border: tall $panel;
}


/* =============================================================================
   Select (format picker, level picker)
   =============================================================================
   Textual's Select renders three components: the closed widget, the
   current value, and the open overlay. All three are styled here so
   that the closed state and the open state share the same background
   and border — the overlay would otherwise inherit the surface colour
   and disappear against the surrounding panel.
   ============================================================================= */

Select {
    background: $surface;
    border: tall $primary;
}

Select:focus {
    border: tall $accent;
}

SelectCurrent {
    background: $surface;
    color: $text;
    border: none;
}

SelectOverlay {
    background: $panel;
    border: tall $primary;
}

SelectOverlay > .option-list--option {
    padding: 0 1;
}

SelectOverlay > .option-list--option-highlighted {
    background: $primary;
    color: $text;
}


/* =============================================================================
   OptionList (main menu, settings sections, help categories)
   =============================================================================
   The list is rendered with a panel background so it reads as a
   distinct sidebar. The highlighted row uses the primary colour when
   the list does not have focus, and the accent colour (with bold text)
   when it does — the user can see at a glance which widget is
   receiving keyboard input.
   ============================================================================= */

OptionList {
    background: $panel;
    color: $text;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
}

OptionList > .option-list--option {
    padding: 0 1;
    color: $text;
}

OptionList > .option-list--option-highlighted {
    background: $primary;
    color: $text;
}

OptionList:focus > .option-list--option-highlighted {
    background: $accent;
    color: $text;
    text-style: bold;
}

OptionList > .option-list--option-disabled {
    color: $text-disabled;
}


/* =============================================================================
   DataTable (chapter list)
   =============================================================================
   The chapter list relies on zebra striping to help the eye track a
   row across the four columns. **Zebra striping is enabled by the
   widget** (``zebra_stripes=True`` on the ``DataTable`` constructor)
   — Textual 3.x does not expose the stripe colours as CSS classes,
   so this file cannot override them. The colours below are what the
   theme's ``$surface`` / ``$panel`` / ``$boost`` produce by default;
   if a deployment wants different zebra colours, it must pass them
   to the ``DataTable`` constructor via ``DataTable(zebra_stripes=True)``
   with a custom colour scheme (not supported by the widget today —
   the reference is here for a future change).

   The cursor row must remain visible when the table does not have
   focus (a user comparing two chapters before clicking) and must be
   more prominent when it does.
   ============================================================================= */

DataTable {
    background: $surface;
    color: $text;
}

DataTable > .datatable--header {
    background: $panel;
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold;
}

DataTable > .datatable--cursor {
    background: $primary;
    color: $text;
}

DataTable:focus > .datatable--cursor {
    background: $accent;
    color: $text;
    text-style: bold;
}

DataTable > .datatable--hover {
    background: $boost;
}


/* =============================================================================
   RichLog (log viewer body)
   =============================================================================
   RichLog renders pre-styled Rich Text. The background and colour
   properties here only affect the padding area around the log body;
   the per-line styling comes from the LogEntry renderer in
   ``log_viewer.py``.
   ============================================================================= */

RichLog {
    background: $surface;
    color: $text;
    padding: 0;
}


/* =============================================================================
   Textual's built-in ProgressBar (overall bar in the download screen)
   =============================================================================
   The overall bar is styled with the accent colour so it reads as the
   primary progress indicator. The per-task bars (Unicode block
   characters in ``progress_bar.py``) are styled separately by their
   own builder.
   ============================================================================= */

ProgressBar {
    color: $accent;
}

ProgressBar > .progress-bar--bar {
    color: $primary;
}

ProgressBar > .progress-bar--percentage {
    color: $text-muted;
}


/* =============================================================================
   Modal screens (the quit-confirmation dialog)
   =============================================================================
   A modal is rendered on top of the screen beneath it with a dimmed
   backdrop. The dim is expressed as an ``rgba()`` colour with a
   fractional alpha — Textual 3.x does not accept the shorthand
   ``$background 60%`` that appears in older Textual documentation;
   ``rgba()`` with a literal or variable colour is the supported form.

   The 60% alpha is a stylistic choice: a lighter dim (30%) would
   leave more of the underlying screen visible, a heavier one (80%)
   would feel claustrophobic. 60% matches the modal styling of most
   modern TUIs.
   ============================================================================= */

ModalScreen {
    background: $background 60%;
    align: center middle;
}

/* Note: the ``$background 60%`` syntax above is what Textual
   3.x's parser accepts in a TCSS file for a semi-transparent colour.
   The alternative ``rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6)`` also works but loses the
   theme-variable linkage — a deployment that swaps to
   ``textual-light`` would still see a black backdrop. */


/* =============================================================================
   Scrollbars
   =============================================================================
   Every scrollable container in the app (VerticalScroll on most
   screens, RichLog in the log viewer, OptionList in the main menu)
   inherits this scrollbar styling. The universal selector is used
   deliberately — the ``scrollbar-*`` properties are silently ignored
   by non-scrollable widgets, and the alternative (an explicit list of
   every scrollable widget class) would have to be updated every time
   a new scrollable widget is introduced.

   The neutral state is dim (the scrollbar should not compete with
   content for attention), the hover and active states are accent, and
   the track is the panel colour so the scrollbar reads as part of the
   surrounding chrome.
   ============================================================================= */

* {
    scrollbar-background: $panel;
    scrollbar-background-hover: $boost;
    scrollbar-background-active: $boost;
    scrollbar-color: $primary;
    scrollbar-color-hover: $accent;
    scrollbar-color-active: $accent;
    scrollbar-corner-color: $panel;
}


/* =============================================================================
   Loading indicator
   =============================================================================
   Textual's built-in LoadingIndicator is displayed briefly during any
   worker that runs longer than the App's loading delay (default 0.5s).
   The accent colour matches the rest of the app's "this is doing
   something" language.
   ============================================================================= */

LoadingIndicator {
    color: $accent;
}


/* =============================================================================
   Badges used in manga card titles and chapter-list status cells
   =============================================================================
   The manga card builder in ``manga_card.py`` emits styled Text spans
   using literal Rich style strings (``"bold red"`` for the adult
   badge, ``"bold cyan"`` for the current-chapter status, ``"green"``
   for read). The ``$nsdl-*`` variables declared at the top of this
   file are **not yet consumed** — they are the single named home for
   those colours, intended for a future refactor of the widget code
   that replaces the literals with theme-variable lookups. Until that
   refactor lands, the variables are documented here for a themer to
   see, and the widget-level ``Text`` styling remains the source of
   truth for the actual colours.

   The comment marker below is the reminder: when a widget starts
   using ``$nsdl-*``, add a CSS rule (or a documented widget-level
   change) so the variable is not only declared but actually applied.
   ============================================================================= */

/* The spans themselves are rendered by the widget; no CSS rules apply. */
/* TODO(future): migrate manga_card.py and chapter_list.py from literal
   Rich style strings to symbolic references backed by the $nsdl-*
   variables above. */
